    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d216">

                <pb facs="d212-02" n="2" sameAs="frus:frus1969-76ve06/d212-02"/>

                <head>216. Telegram S27073 From the Secretary’s Delegation in Tanzania to the
                    Embassy in Nigeria<anchor n="1" xml:id="d216-f1"/>
                    <anchor n="2" xml:id="d216-f2"/>
                </head>
                <dateline><placeName>Dar es Salaam</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                        when="1976-09-15T13:32:00+00:00">September 15, 1976, 1332Z</date>
                </dateline>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>Subject:</label>
                    <item>Secretary’s Possible Visit to Nigeria</item>
                </list>
                <list type="references">
                    <head>Ref:</head>
                    <item>Lagos 10349</item>
                </list>
                <p>1. You should not raise again subject of lunch in Washington and if Garba or Mea
                    raises it you should state that you will have to refer matter to Washington.</p>
                <p>2. Whatever Garba’s personal position regarding Secretary’s visit we see no
                    advantage, after second rejection from FMG, to giving Garba impression that we
                    can somehow retain image of normal relationship, separating his relationship
                    from the action of the government he represents.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed> Kissinger </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d216-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d216-f1-content">
                <p>Source: National Archives, RG 59, Central Policy Files, 1976. Secret; Immediate;
                    Nodis. Repeated to the Department.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d216-f2" n="2" xml:id="d216-f2-content">
                <p> Kissinger, traveling in Tanzania, said that after a second Nigerian rejection of
                    his proposed visit, he could not give the impression of a normal relationship
                    between the two countries.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
